1
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:07,693
Every field against every field, taken apart in depth.

2
00:00:08,493 --> 00:00:13,962
Matrices fold the records that pass the filters, during the same scan as everything else.

3
00:00:14,762 --> 00:00:19,826
You name a battery of numeric fields once as a vector, then ask for a matrix over it.

4
00:00:20,626 --> 00:00:28,911
Each result carries the matrix, extra figures, and any data-quality warnings. It arrives at the end of the run, never row by row.

5
00:00:29,711 --> 00:00:38,743
Its components count the rows used and dropped. Our cohort is three hundred illustrative survey rows; every figure is a real Pulse result.

6
00:00:39,543 --> 00:00:45,162
A vector lists its members by name, by glob, or by pattern, with optional display labels.

7
00:00:45,962 --> 00:00:53,799
Schema members are numeric. Yes-or-no fields join when coerced to zero and one. Categories and dates are refused.

8
00:00:54,599 --> 00:00:59,279
Predict echoes the resolved members, so you can check a glob before you trust it.

9
00:01:00,079 --> 00:01:07,319
One native vector field can hold the whole battery. Name it directly, and pick elements as items one, items two.

10
00:01:08,119 --> 00:01:15,828
A member can also be computed in the same request, here a formula that fills the blank V three. Now no row is dropped.

11
00:01:16,628 --> 00:01:23,804
Standardize turns a covariance into a correlation, and records encoding sends every part as row-shaped records.

12
00:01:24,604 --> 00:01:28,239
The correlation matrix puts every member against every other.

13
00:01:29,039 --> 00:01:36,727
Here two blocks stand out: the service items move together, and so do the value items. Top pairs lists the strongest cells.

14
00:01:37,527 --> 00:01:43,786
The covariance matrix is the same grid in the fields' own units, with variances on the diagonal.

15
00:01:44,586 --> 00:01:49,415
Upper encoding sends half the grid. An undefined cell is null, never zero.

16
00:01:50,215 --> 00:01:56,474
One item is blank on thirty rows. Listwise, the default, drops those rows from every cell.

17
00:01:57,274 --> 00:02:03,063
Pairwise lets each cell use every row where both members are present, and reports each pair's count.

18
00:02:03,863 --> 00:02:13,492
But a pairwise grid can stop being a valid correlation matrix. Partial correlation, PCA, factor analysis and collinearity then refuse it.

19
00:02:14,292 --> 00:02:21,169
Reliability keeps alpha and returns omega as null, with a warning. Or ask for the nearest valid repair.

20
00:02:21,969 --> 00:02:24,601
Pearson reads values along a straight line.

21
00:02:25,401 --> 00:02:31,041
Spearman and Kendall read ranks instead, which suits ordinal scales, outliers and curves.

22
00:02:31,841 --> 00:02:37,460
Ranks need every row, so these run buffered and serially, and take frequency weights only.

23
00:02:38,260 --> 00:02:43,580
Partial correlation asks what is left of each link once the other members are held fixed.

24
00:02:44,380 --> 00:02:51,087
S one and S two drop from point seven four to point five two. Part of their link runs through S three.

25
00:02:51,887 --> 00:02:57,122
You can choose which fields to control for. Collinear fields are refused as singular.

26
00:02:57,922 --> 00:03:03,541
Reliability asks whether several items measure one scale. Here R four is reverse-keyed.

27
00:03:04,341 --> 00:03:12,285
Left as it is, alpha collapses to point one nine. A low or negative alpha usually means an item was not reversed.

28
00:03:13,085 --> 00:03:18,426
Reverse it on its one-to-seven range, and alpha and omega both reach point eight nine.

29
00:03:19,226 --> 00:03:23,074
Per item, dropping any one would lower alpha, so all four stay.

30
00:03:23,874 --> 00:03:32,053
The range is required unless the vector declares scale bounds. Omega can come back null with a warning; alpha always computes.

31
00:03:32,853 --> 00:03:36,786
Principal components ask how many dimensions carry the variance.

32
00:03:37,586 --> 00:03:44,527
Two eigenvalues sit above one, so Kaiser's rule keeps two components, with seventy-seven percent of the variance.

33
00:03:45,327 --> 00:03:50,498
The loadings show service items on the first component and value items on the second.

34
00:03:51,298 --> 00:03:58,858
KMO and Bartlett say the battery is worth factoring. Varimax rotation sharpens the pattern, and signs are free.

35
00:03:59,658 --> 00:04:04,957
PCA summarizes variance. A model of hidden causes is factor analysis.

36
00:04:05,757 --> 00:04:10,672
Factor analysis models each item as the echo of a few hidden qualities.

37
00:04:11,472 --> 00:04:17,603
The loadings say how strongly each item reflects its factor, and the uniqueness is what the factors miss.

38
00:04:18,403 --> 00:04:22,507
Maximum likelihood adds fit figures. Here two factors fit well.

39
00:04:23,307 --> 00:04:29,288
A uniqueness at or below zero, or too many factors for the items, comes back with a warning.

40
00:04:30,088 --> 00:04:34,704
Collinearity checks whether candidate predictors are too alike to fit together.

41
00:04:35,504 --> 00:04:41,037
Age and tenure correlate at point nine eight, so their variance inflation factors pass twenty.

42
00:04:41,837 --> 00:04:44,768
Belsley's condition indices point to the same pair.

43
00:04:45,568 --> 00:04:51,997
Exactly dependent predictors are refused as singular, with the fields named. Drop or combine them.

44
00:04:52,797 --> 00:04:57,072
Distance measures how far apart the centres of a grouper's buckets lie.

45
00:04:57,872 --> 00:05:03,277
Standardized, Gold rates service higher and value lower, so it sits furthest from Bronze.

46
00:05:04,077 --> 00:05:10,080
Any grouper but a date grouper can split the rows, including an embedder's own, and you choose the metric.

47
00:05:10,880 --> 00:05:14,280
Add groups, and every matrix comes back once per bucket.

48
00:05:15,080 --> 00:05:21,232
Each bucket equals the ungrouped matrix over exactly its rows. Compare them as separate answers.

49
00:05:22,032 --> 00:05:27,181
Predict estimates the buckets and bytes first, and leaves the estimate out when it cannot know.

50
00:05:27,981 --> 00:05:37,482
Matrices can carry p-values. Turn on inference, and correlation and partial correlation add a p-value and a confidence interval for every pair.

51
00:05:38,282 --> 00:05:46,823
A multiplicity block corrects the fifteen pairs as one matrix family. Every within-block pair survives Holm; no cross-block pair does.

52
00:05:47,623 --> 00:05:54,180
Structural equation models report a p-value for every estimate, with their own block. They get their own film.

53
00:05:54,980 --> 00:06:00,428
Vectors also feed profile tests, row scores and clusters, and group aggregates.

54
00:06:01,228 --> 00:06:06,015
The coefficient covariance of a fitted model lives on the regression, not here.

55
00:06:06,815 --> 00:06:12,306
Predict reports each matrix's shape, memory and streamability before any row is read.

56
00:06:13,106 --> 00:06:15,333
A rank method buffers every row.

57
00:06:16,133 --> 00:06:25,314
Streaming matrices merge in fixed blocks, so serial, parallel and sharded runs are bit-identical. A failed matrix slot fails alone.

58
00:06:26,114 --> 00:06:32,394
That's the toolkit today: grids, scales and structure, checks and segments, and the knobs that tune them.

59
00:06:33,194 --> 00:06:42,055
More are added over time, and the manifest shows what your instance offers. Embedders can register their own through options dot extensions.

60
00:06:42,855 --> 00:06:48,751
Remember: check the members and the n before you read a cell. Next up, structural equation models.

61
00:06:49,551 --> 00:06:50,903
Pulse matrices.
